From: Luma Developers
Subject: [PATCH] Keycap: a press costs a translation, and the keyboard serves the unlock prompt

Measured on the Pixel 9 Pro Fold, handling a TOUCH_BEGIN took 7.1 ms and
the press frame 13 ms of redraw. Instrumented headless at 360x808 scale 3
the press path spent its time measuring every key (get_transformed_extents
on 33 keys), in word-model lookups, in the cap's :active restyle, in setting
the preview's text and in building the haptic call. Now:

- Key cells (whole cell and cap) are cached per layer allocation in layer
  coordinates; a press maps the touch once (transform_stage_point) and reads
  one stage position.
- Letter priors for near-boundary touches are computed in an idle after the
  word changes, never on a press.
- A press restyles nothing: the cap travels by translation, a single tint
  actor moves over it, and each character has its own preview label that is
  only moved and shown, in one layer above the UI.
- Haptics send one prebuilt fire-and-forget D-Bus message right after the
  frame that shows the press; the key path reads cached preferences.
- Surrounding-text reports read only the tail before the caret, however long
  the document.

TOUCH_BEGIN handling headless: median 0.12 ms (was 1.5 ms), p90 0.24 ms.

On the lock screen the keyboard serves only a visible Shell entry: locking
closes a keyboard opened for an application, application text-input requests
do not open it, and the unlock prompt's entry opens it once the curtain is
lifted and the entry is on screen. The unlock content is laid out above an
open keyboard, so the compact Prairie prompt stays visible.

